    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d242">

                <pb facs="0608" n="586" xml:id="pg586"/>

                <head>242. Memorandum of a Telephone Conversation Between the Secretary of State and
                    the Deputy Under Secretary of State for Political Affairs (Johnson)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d242-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                ><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                                type="conversation-or-meeting-date" when="1961-11-13T14:08:00-05:00"
                                >November 13, 1961, 2:08 p.m.</date></hi></dateline></opener>
                <p>[Here follows the beginning of the conversation regarding Laos.]</p>
                <p>Mr. Johnson asked if the Sec. had gotten the message from
                    the NSC on Vietnam.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d242-f2"/> Johnson said he had tried to
                    discourage General Taylor and according
                    to Taylor the Pres. was receptive. Johnson said he had told <hi rendition="#italic">Taylor</hi> he
                    thought we had our decisions and that there was no necessity for another
                    meeting. Mr. Johnson said that Gen. Taylor was on his way to talk to the Vice
                    President now. The only ones left out were the Vice President and Dillon.
                        Johnson said he had had word that
                        McNamara was agreeable if the
                    Secretary was. He said we could check the paper out with Dillon and Murrow without having a meeting. The Sec. asked
                    if they had indicated they wanted to wait until after NSC mtg., adding he would
                    talk to Taylor by phone.<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d242-f3"/></p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d242-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d242-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Rusk Files:
                    Lot 72 D 192, Telephone Calls. Transcribed by Mildred Asbjornson of the Office
                    of the Secretary.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d242-f2" n="2" xml:id="d242-f2-content">
                <p>Not further identified, but it apparently pertained to the scheduling of another
                    meeting on Vietnam.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d242-f3" n="3" xml:id="d242-f3-content">
                <p>At 2:15 p.m., Rusk called Johnson, who said
                    “we would have to move quickly-Galbraith had been working hard on this.” Johnson
                    said he was “a little worried” since he had given Galbraith a copy of the draft instructions to be sent to
                        Nolting. (Department of State,
                        Rusk Files: Lot 72 D 192, Telephone
                    Calls) For Rusk’s subsequent telephone
                    conversation with Taylor, see <ref
                        target="#d244">Document 244</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
